WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:06.319
Ben Wattenberg: Hello.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:07.319
I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:12.610
On July 4, 1776, our Founding Fathers cut
America’s ties with England by adopting

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.309
the Declaration of Independence.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:20.630
The author of their manifesto was only 33
years old: Thomas Jefferson, philosopher,

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.199
diplomat, president, slaveholder, and sometime
radical.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:28.839
Who was Thomas Jefferson, and what would he
think of our nation today?

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:34.220
Joining us to sort through the conflict and
the consensus are Judge Robert Bork of the

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:39.129
American Enterprise Institute and author of
“The Tempting of America”; Professor Peter

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:44.320
Onuf, the holder of the Thomas Jefferson Memorial
Foundation chair at the University of Virginia

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.180
and editor of “Jefferson Legacies”; James
Oliver Horton, a scholar of the Jeffersonian

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.970
era, professor of history and American studies
at George Washington University, and author

00:00:54.970 --> 00:01:01.090
of “Free People of Color: Inside the African
American Community”; and Jan Lewis of Rutgers

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:06.610
University and author of “The Pursuit of
Happiness: Family and Values in Jefferson’s

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:07.610
Virginia.”

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.159
The question before this house: What about
Jefferson?

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:29.350
This week on “Think Tank.”

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:35.490
In 1962, at a dinner honoring Nobel Prize
winners, President John F. Kennedy said, “I

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:41.009
think this is the most extraordinary collection
of talent and of human knowledge that has

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:45.970
ever been gathered together at the White House,
with the possible exception of when Thomas

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.710
Jefferson dined alone.”

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.060
Some historians say that Thomas Jefferson
was the last Renaissance man.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.500
The author of the Declaration of Independence
and third president of the United States spoke

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:05.039
six languages and was an accomplished inventor,
naturalist, mathematician, and architect.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:10.090
Jefferson personally designed his home, Monticello,
as well as the University of Virginia.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.170
Jefferson’s greatest legacy, of course,
is the Declaration of Independence.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:20.500
It is probably the single most recognized
and copied political document in the world.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.910
Its powerful words still resonate everywhere:
“We hold these truths to be self-evident,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:32.600
that all men are created equal, that they
are endowed by their creator with certain

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:38.450
inalienable rights, that among these are life,
liberty and the pursuit of happiness.”

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:45.110
Now, how could the man who wrote those stirring
words own 150 slaves?

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.660
And unlike George Washington, who freed all
of his slaves upon his death, Jefferson freed

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.720
only five.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.470
But he thought slavery would not last, declaring,
“Nothing is more certainly written in the

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:02.630
Book of Faith than that these people are to
be free.”

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.019
Political life was not easy for Jefferson.

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:10.340
His hatred of aristocracy made him a strong
supporter of the French Revolution.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:16.640
His enemies charged that he was too fond of
popular uprisings, calling him “Mad Tom”

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.080
and “the Robespierre of the American mob.”

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.699
But supporters hailed him as “the mammoth
of democracy” and as “the man of the people.”

00:03:26.699 --> 00:03:30.420
He died on July 4, 1826.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:36.950
On his tomb, he asked to be remembered as
“the author of the Declaration of Independence,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:42.860
the founder of the University of Virginia,
and author of the Virginia Statute of Religious

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.260
Freedom.”

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:47.750
Professor Onuf — Peter Onuf, let’s start
out.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.400
What kind of a man was he?

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.980
Peter Onuf: Jefferson was an extraordinary
man, as your list of his achievements indicates.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.650
We still don’t know what to make of him,
though.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:01.600
I think the notion of Renaissance man is a
kind of fudge.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.520
He’s everything, which means that we don’t
really know what he is.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.759
What’s most remarkable about Jefferson the
democrat is that if anybody represents the

00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:16.350
aristocratic impulses in America before the
Revolution, it’s Jefferson and his class

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.400
in Virginia.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.880
In effect, he’s the cream of the Virginia
aristocracy, and it’s remarkable that he

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.390
could embrace these ideas that we now take
to be the defining ideas of democracy.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.430
Ben Wattenberg: Jim Horton?

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:33.680
James Horton: Jefferson is an extraordinary
man — was an extraordinary man.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.370
He was also a very complicated man.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:42.790
In that regard, I think he becomes the personification,
really, of American society.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:50.410
He is a person of great principle, but a person
who cannot always achieve that which his principles

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:51.410
would dictate.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:55.430
I think the same can be said of our society
— great principles, but we don’t always

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.430
live up to them.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.580
Ben Wattenberg: Jan Lewis?

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:02.980
Jan Lewis: I’d like to go back to a point
that Peter made and talk about this conundrum

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:08.310
or paradox that Jefferson is the exponent
of democracy, yet in many ways he comes from

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:09.570
an aristocratic background.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:14.640
Historians, I don’t think, have ever been
able to explain fully that paradox.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:21.930
How a man of such privilege — and he in
many ways maintained privilege in that beautiful

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:29.930
setting at Monticello — how he was able
to go beyond his background and come up with

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:36.270
ideas that are supposed to apply to all people
and that all people have chosen as their banner.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:40.750
Ben Wattenberg: Bob Bork, Judge Bork, lawyer
Bork.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.780
And you are surrounded by historians here.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.350
What do you make of Jefferson as a lawgiver,
law writer?

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:51.480
I mean, that’s what he is principally known
as, I guess.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.980
Robert Bork: Well, if you mean the Declaration
of Independence, of course that is really

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.620
in part a propaganda document; it’s a rhetorical
document.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.260
It’s not really part of our law.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.770
I suppose to the Virginia Statute of Religious
Freedom was a great achievement, but I don’t

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.470
think of Jefferson primarily as a lawgiver.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:10.770
Ben Wattenberg: Why not?

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:11.770
I mean, he —

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.200
Robert Bork: Well, for one thing, you know,
if Jefferson’s principles had prevailed,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.590
they would have destroyed the United States.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:17.910
The Union would have been gone.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:23.820
He thought, for example, that the states could
nullify acts of Congress if they thought they

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.580
were — if each state thought it was unconstitutional.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:27.910
He didn’t believe in judicial review.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:32.530
He thought each branch of government could
decide constitutionality for itself.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:36.460
Ben Wattenberg: That was the great fight with
Chief Justice Marshall?

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.590
Robert Bork: Yes, that was part of the fight
with Marshall.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.940
He wanted a new Constitutional Convention
every 20 years.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.130
James Madison talked him out of that one.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:49.920
But obviously, if these principles had prevailed,
we would have —the states would have fragmented,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:50.920
and the Union would have been destroyed.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:54.380
Peter Onuf: I think it’s only fair to say,
Bob, that if Hamilton’s principles had prevailed,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.980
the same thing would have happened.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.050
Robert Bork: Well, that could well be.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.200
Ben Wattenberg: Was Jefferson a radical?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.500
Is that fair to say?

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:05.490
I mean, all this stuff about “watering the
tree of liberty with human blood every 20

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:06.490
years,” that sort of stuff?

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.480
Peter Onuf: Well, I think some of his rhetoric
was radical.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:17.240
And just to get back to the point you were
making here, his notions about being able

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.990
to review — each state being able to review
and to decide congressional rulings for themselves.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:29.240
I mean, that comes in the context of the fear
that he had that the Federalists at the time

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:34.700
of the Sedition Act were really concentrating
power in the hands of the federal government

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.290
to a dangerous extent.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.870
So in some ways, that was a reaction.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:44.020
I’m not sure that if the context had been
different, he would have held to that belief.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:48.300
Robert Bork: Well, certainly when he interpreted
the Constitution, he interpreted federal power

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.760
very narrowly, and this would have been a
very different nation if he had prevailed.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:57.040
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask you a question.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:03.920
If Thomas Jefferson were alive today, would
he be a Democrat or a Republican, Jan?

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.290
Jan Lewis: My son — I have to introduce
this by saying, my son, who is 16 and was

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:15.680
attending many of the festivities around Jefferson’s
250th birthday last year, got so frustrated

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.470
with this question that he insisted, if Thomas
Jefferson were alive today, he’d be 250

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:21.470
years old.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:22.470
[Laughter.]

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:31.710
And I think I have to resist that sort of
question because the times were so extraordinarily

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:32.710
different.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:37.890
That much said, I will say that we can follow
different parts of the Jeffersonian legacy,

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.839
and they go in very different directions.

00:08:40.839 --> 00:08:47.440
As Bob has suggested, there is a states’
rights Jefferson that leads to nullification

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.590
and to the Southern side of the Civil War,
and you can see those ideas developing.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:57.700
At the same time, there is Jefferson the rights
giver, and who leads to the sort of rights

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.220
consciousness that we associate with liberal
Democrats.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:06.140
So there are different parts of his legacy
that lead in very different directions.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:11.070
Ben Wattenberg: How could a man who — I
mean, at times when you read about him, he

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.470
sounds like sort of an agrarian nut.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.839
I mean, he hates cities.

00:09:14.839 --> 00:09:15.839
He hates mobs.

00:09:15.839 --> 00:09:17.360
He hates people in cities.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.339
How could a man like that be a member of the
Democratic Party today?

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:21.810
James Horton: He distrusts cities.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:25.540
Peter Onuf: I think that’s misleading, in
any case, that notion of his hating cities.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.520
He’s a very urbane man.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.440
He loves urbanity and civility — that is,
the good things that cities bring.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:39.500
His political project is in opposition to
the metropolis, the concentration of power

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:45.170
that he resisted in the patriotic resistance
movement before the Revolution, and the danger

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:50.450
that such concentrations would be — emerge
in America under the aegis of a Hamiltonian

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.450
system.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.660
James Horton: And to show you how complex
and how contradictory he was, you’re absolutely

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.950
right that he feared the concentration of
federal power.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.242
But he used federal power when he was in the
federal office, when he was president of the

00:10:03.242 --> 00:10:07.360
United States — for example, the Louisiana
Purchase, which expanded the size of the United

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.390
States several times.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:09.900
Ben Wattenberg: And which was unconstitutional.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:14.100
James Horton: Which was — at least he was
not sure that it was constitutional and said

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.570
that, that he was not sure it was constitutional.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.089
Ben Wattenberg: Judge Bork, was it unconstitutional?

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:21.350
Robert Bork: Probably was, probably was, but
there was a great disconnect between Jefferson’s

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.020
words and his actions as president.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:26.630
In fact, when he was president, he had to
evade all of the principles that he had laid

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.390
down before he became president.

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:32.940
Not only the Louisiana Purchase, but he sent
out the Navy and the Marine Corps to Tripoli

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.580
to attack the Barbary pirates.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:35.900
Ben Wattenberg: Without getting congressional
approval.

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:36.900
Robert Bork: That’s right.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.890
One of the earliest precedents for a president
initiating hostilities without a declaration

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:41.890
of war.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:47.420
Ben Wattenberg: What about his attitude toward
race and slavery?

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:50.660
How do we deal with that now?

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:58.010
James Horton: Jefferson — well, he was obviously
a slaveholder, obviously a man who was uncomfortable

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:04.250
with the institution of slavery, who thought
the institution of slavery was wrong, and

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:08.100
even so, could not bring himself to free his
own slaves.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:15.020
He only freed eight slaves his entire — well,
three during his lifetime, five in his will.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:22.720
A man with some 200 slaves who never moved
to the position of liberalism on the question

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:28.220
of slavery that many in his own time, in his
own region did.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:37.180
So that although he was in some ways a leader
in terms of democratic thinking, he was — he

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:42.589
lagged behind more progressive thinking on
the question of slavery, even for his time

00:11:42.589 --> 00:11:43.589
period.

00:11:43.589 --> 00:11:47.420
Robert Bork: Well, I think early on he proposed
a measure that no slavery would be allowed

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:51.110
in new states, and he lost that by one vote
in the Congress.

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:56.050
James Horton: Sure, but when the new states
were actually opening up — 1820s — he

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.750
went exactly in the opposite direction.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:04.720
He denounced all that, said that slavery ought
to be allowed wherever it would expand.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:09.200
Peter Onuf: I think we have to understand
the limits of the notion of equality, and

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:14.160
to some extent, we have to accept that when
Jefferson articulates universal principles,

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.580
he comes up against a difference which he
can’t accommodate in his scheme.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:25.970
That is, it’s one of the aspects of liberalism
to generalize and universalize rights that

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:30.520
we define ever more clearly the boundary between
those who are capable of bearing rights and

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.760
those who are not.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.750
This is where Jefferson’s racism comes to
the fore — what we would call his racism.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:37.570
He naturalized difference.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:42.060
He couldn’t accept artificial difference
— that is, aristocracy — where privileged

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:44.399
families would rule over others.

00:12:44.399 --> 00:12:49.720
But there were differences, for instance,
in the family, between men and women, parents

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.930
and children, between races.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.260
He believed that African Americans were naturally
inferior.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:59.940
Jan Lewis: Yeah, I think when we read his
statement in the “Notes from the State of

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:06.490
Virginia” about blacks, they make us extremely
uncomfortable today, and that this is a paradox

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:07.490
with Jefferson.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.760
I mean he knows — he absolutely knows that
slavery is wrong.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:15.810
Everyone at the time knows that Jefferson
is opposed to slavery, particularly at the

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.240
time of the Revolution.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:21.810
There is no question where the Declaration
of Independence leads: that it will lead to

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.710
the elimination of slavery.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:30.110
At the same time, Jefferson plays around with
and creates a racism today that makes us,

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:33.110
I think, very uncomfortable when we read those
words.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:38.600
Ben Wattenberg: Did Thomas Jefferson have
an affair with Sally Hemings, a slave on his

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:39.600
plantation?

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.620
James Horton: Obviously, we don’t know the
answer to that question.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:46.220
There is lots of circumstantial evidence that
he did, but there is no smoking gun.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:50.529
I don’t know of anybody who could testify,
“Yes, I know for sure that he did.”

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:54.250
Peter Onuf: The only argument I would make
against the relationship — and I don’t

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:58.410
like to put myself in the position of defending
Jefferson on this, but I think the main reason

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:03.110
why it’s not likely to have happened is
that he was such a racist.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:09.620
And for him, miscegenation, mixing the races,
blurring the line between black and white

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.470
created this unbearable dissonance and tension.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:17.820
Many of the people in the Hemings connection
were light-skinned; they could have passed

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:18.820
for white.

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:22.680
And to perpetuate that confusion of the races
while you’re claiming that the races are

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:27.870
so distinct in their capabilities is to make
an unbearable situation for

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:31.800
James Horton: Yes, but it would have been
very much in keeping with the kind of ethical

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:36.709
dilemmas and inconsistency that we know Jefferson
had routinely in his life.

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:42.860
Ben Wattenberg: Jan, you have written about
family life of Jefferson and his times.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.100
Where do you come out on the great Sally Hemings
mystery?

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:46.810
Jan Lewis: Oh, I blame the nephews.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:47.870
Ben Wattenberg: You blame the nephews.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:48.920
Jan Lewis: The family, in fact.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:52.830
Ben Wattenberg: Well, wasn’t there one child
that was born exactly nine months after he

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.880
returns as ambassador to France?

00:14:54.880 --> 00:15:02.980
Jan Lewis: Well, the timing is thought that
Jefferson might have been the father — that

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:07.740
he was in the vicinity nine months before
the birth of each of Sally Hemings’ children.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.600
Ben Wattenberg: But so were the nephews, you’re
saying?

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:11.970
Jan Lewis: No one’s done the research on
the nephews.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:17.779
The family thought that it was the nephews,
and in fact, many years afterwards, they would

00:15:17.779 --> 00:15:20.480
say, well, we know it was Uncle Peter.

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.510
It was Uncle Sam.

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:29.700
And what I found significant about that is
that even if Thomas Jefferson himself were

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:34.820
not the father of Sally Hemings’ children,
which I think I would agree with Peter — not

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:37.370
likely.

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:38.779
Could be, but not likely.

00:15:38.779 --> 00:15:45.850
Even if he weren’t, by the family’s own
admission, these children would have been

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:48.350
Jefferson’s great-nephews and nieces.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:49.550
They were family.

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:54.850
And I think that this is a metaphor for race
relations in this country in that we’re

00:15:54.850 --> 00:16:00.250
all in some way family, and we should all
acknowledge that we are all related, that

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:05.860
all of us are the descendants of Thomas Jefferson,
if not literally, metaphorically.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:10.560
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask a question here.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:17.570
Here you have — as we’re talking here,
you have a man who certainly on the race issue

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:24.300
does not seem like the great exemplar and
trumpeter of human liberty.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:32.769
And yet around the world — forget about
America for a minute —Jefferson, not Washington,

00:16:32.769 --> 00:16:38.240
not Hamilton, is — every revolutionary in
the world, the good guys as well as the totalitarians,

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:43.010
waves the Declaration of Independence and
says, “All men are created equal,” “self-determination.”

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:46.029
Where is that hypocrisy?

00:16:46.029 --> 00:16:47.029
I mean —

00:16:47.029 --> 00:16:52.399
Robert Bork: Well, Forrest McDonald, who was
an historian, said that Jefferson combined

00:16:52.399 --> 00:17:00.649
deviousness, slipperiness, hypocrisy with
charm and grace and a sure sense of his own

00:17:00.649 --> 00:17:05.740
purposes, which are priceless assets in a
politician.

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:07.169
So that he did say a lot of —

00:17:07.169 --> 00:17:08.220
Ben Wattenberg: Does that remind you of anybody?

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:09.220
[Laughter.]

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.069
James Horton: Well, you know, here you have
the difference between his actions and his

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:13.350
words.

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:18.579
There is a great reverence within 19th-century
black society for his words.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:24.239
The conventions of blacks that were held throughout
the 1830s and the 1840s started off every

00:17:24.239 --> 00:17:27.009
meeting with the reading of the Declaration
of Independence.

00:17:27.009 --> 00:17:31.440
So that in terms of his words, they were revered
within the black community because, after

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:36.609
all, those black people did believe all men
were created equal and so on.

00:17:36.609 --> 00:17:37.609
Actions —

00:17:37.609 --> 00:17:40.679
Robert Bork: He was a great rhetorician, and
he got off a lot of phrases that today we

00:17:40.679 --> 00:17:45.330
would identify with the new left, which is
why I think a lot of his rhetoric is so popular

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.799
in an egalitarian age.

00:17:47.799 --> 00:17:49.010
But he was not an egalitarian.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:53.351
He referred to the people — when he opposed
the idea of direct election of senators, he

00:17:53.351 --> 00:17:55.230
referred to the people as “the swinish multitude.”

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:57.620
Ben Wattenberg: “The swinish multitude.”

00:17:57.620 --> 00:17:59.450
That’s a pretty good bumper sticker for
a politician.

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:05.019
Peter Onuf: I think the problem is not formulated
properly when we talk about the disjunction

00:18:05.019 --> 00:18:07.100
between words and actions.

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:09.190
I think we’re looking at the outside in.

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:10.260
We’re saying, what are the boundaries?

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.140
How far do you go, Jefferson?

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:13.149
Aha, we’ve found you.

00:18:13.149 --> 00:18:14.149
We’ve caught you out.

00:18:14.149 --> 00:18:15.230
These are the limits.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:16.490
You can’t be serious.

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:17.490
This is just nonsense.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:24.039
But if we start with Jefferson’s own experience
of his world and talk about what his aspirations

00:18:24.039 --> 00:18:26.850
were, then it looks differently.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.880
Take the whole idea of equality.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:30.799
Equality is the buzzword of the revolutionary
period.

00:18:30.799 --> 00:18:36.500
Now we have appropriated it for our own multifarious
purposes now, but for Jefferson and his fellow

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:41.649
patriot leaders, equality refers specifically
to their status and the status of their colonies

00:18:41.649 --> 00:18:42.929
in the British Empire.

00:18:42.929 --> 00:18:45.230
It was grounded in the notion of English rights.

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:46.649
It was very specific.

00:18:46.649 --> 00:18:51.999
And what Jefferson did was try to reach beyond
that — not as far as we would like him to,

00:18:51.999 --> 00:18:57.070
perhaps, but the idea that you could form
even a union of liberty-loving, free Americans

00:18:57.070 --> 00:19:01.179
was itself an act of faith, an intellectual
leap.

00:19:01.179 --> 00:19:07.629
So if we look at it from Jefferson’s perspective,
inside out, then I think we have rather a

00:19:07.629 --> 00:19:14.929
different view than if we take this perspective
of why isn’t he good enough for us today?

00:19:14.929 --> 00:19:17.869
Why doesn’t he do what we do, we politically
correct moderns?

00:19:17.869 --> 00:19:20.580
James Horton: But let’s not judge him by
the standards of our time.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:23.009
Let’s judge him by the standards of his
own time.

00:19:23.009 --> 00:19:28.610
There are people in his own time who risked
their careers, who risked their fortunes in

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:33.580
ways that are far more egalitarian than Jefferson
was willing to do.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:40.340
Ben Wattenberg: Let’s move on for a moment
to the legacy of Jefferson.

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:44.909
There is this big argument between Jefferson
and Hamilton about what kind of America are

00:19:44.909 --> 00:19:49.570
we going to have, Jefferson sort of taking
his rural, agrarian view, and Hamilton writing

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.019
the — what is it called about manufacturers?

00:19:52.019 --> 00:19:55.840
“The Report on Manufacturers,” looking
toward a booming, muscular sort of industrial

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:58.370
society.

00:19:58.370 --> 00:20:06.039
Is it fair then to say, given what America
is today, that the true father of this America

00:20:06.039 --> 00:20:07.989
is Hamilton, not Jefferson?

00:20:07.989 --> 00:20:12.639
Jan Lewis: It seems to me that they both are.

00:20:12.639 --> 00:20:21.619
I mean, what we get out of the Hamiltonian
legacy is a strong, active, huge federal government

00:20:21.619 --> 00:20:23.850
— a centralized government.

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:28.019
But we still have also the Jefferson suspicion
of a strong government.

00:20:28.019 --> 00:20:34.800
And it’s in the space between those two,
the actuality of a centralized state and an

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:36.669
American suspicion of a centralized state.

00:20:36.669 --> 00:20:38.519
That’s where American history is made.

00:20:38.519 --> 00:20:41.780
Robert Bork: And I think it’s wrong to think
of either of them as the father of the country.

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:46.070
This country would have developed as it did
if Jefferson and Hamilton had never existed.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:50.350
Jefferson’s idea of an agrarian society
was doomed from the beginning.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:52.919
You couldn’t succeed and live in this world
as an agrarian society.

00:20:52.919 --> 00:20:56.929
I mean, we’d be overrun by other powers.

00:20:56.929 --> 00:21:00.359
And the growth of the central government was
a natural development.

00:21:00.359 --> 00:21:01.359
People wanted it.

00:21:01.359 --> 00:21:05.320
George Washington immediately began proposing
projects that were well outside the federal

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:06.960
power in the Constitution.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:11.200
Ben Wattenberg: So your current argument that
you make these days — you would have been

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:17.809
making that in 1790 — that these guys are
using too much federal power, more than the

00:21:17.809 --> 00:21:18.859
Constitution gave them?

00:21:18.859 --> 00:21:22.170
Robert Bork: Well, the Constitution certainly
doesn’t give the federal government the

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:23.899
power it now has.

00:21:23.899 --> 00:21:27.109
But the Constitution could not stop the federal
power from growing.

00:21:27.109 --> 00:21:28.590
There is no way it could stop it.

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:33.429
I think the strong central government was
in the cards whatever the Constitution said.

00:21:33.429 --> 00:21:40.080
Peter Onuf: I think we misunderstand Jefferson’s
legacy if we think of him simply as an agrarian,

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:47.779
this quaint figure who’s attached to a romanticized
past, who resists modernity and all the ills

00:21:47.779 --> 00:21:48.790
associated with it.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:54.769
I think Jefferson instead — his impact on
American history has been to authorize and

00:21:54.769 --> 00:21:56.529
license private initiative.

00:21:56.529 --> 00:21:58.529
A release of energy.

00:21:58.529 --> 00:22:04.669
His preference for agriculture is a preference
for commercial agriculture and the initiatives

00:22:04.669 --> 00:22:07.929
of individual farmers, and then traders as
well.

00:22:07.929 --> 00:22:12.690
He believed that the force to develop manufacturers,
as Hamilton was going to do, would in fact

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:17.610
be retrograde; it would not contribute to
the wealth of the nation or its future prosperity.

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:21.149
You could argue that through much of the 19th
century, until the great concentrations of

00:22:21.149 --> 00:22:24.899
capital that emerged in the Civil War and
afterwards, that this was the prescription.

00:22:24.899 --> 00:22:27.730
This was the formula that was going to make
America the way it was.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:35.379
So I think there was a choice early on, and
it is significant that the Jeffersonians emerged

00:22:35.379 --> 00:22:37.729
in power with the so-called Revolution of
1800.

00:22:37.729 --> 00:22:42.179
Ben Wattenberg: What about Jefferson and the
world?

00:22:42.179 --> 00:22:47.549
I mean, what would this — what did that
piece of paper mean, that Declaration of Independence?

00:22:47.549 --> 00:22:48.789
Forget America for a minute.

00:22:48.789 --> 00:22:50.229
It’s complicated and we just —

00:22:50.229 --> 00:22:53.249
Peter Onuf: Forget America.

00:22:53.249 --> 00:22:54.249
[Laughter.]

00:22:54.249 --> 00:22:57.779
Ben Wattenberg: Yeah, forget America.

00:22:57.779 --> 00:23:01.509
Bob, is that the revolutionary document of
the world?

00:23:01.509 --> 00:23:07.080
Robert Bork: Well, I think as the world becomes
increasingly egalitarian, the Declaration

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.970
becomes an inspiration to people.

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:11.710
And that’s true I think throughout Western
civilization.

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:15.720
Ben Wattenberg: And it’s been used by good
guys and bad guys?

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:16.960
Robert Bork: Oh, sure.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:21.299
James Horton: But I think that probably the
most important thing here is that it provides

00:23:21.299 --> 00:23:24.749
the touchstone for those people who are oppressed.

00:23:24.749 --> 00:23:29.529
It is a document to which they can turn, to
which they can appeal.

00:23:29.529 --> 00:23:34.600
It becomes a way of appealing to the conscience
of the nation.

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:39.280
And without that document, without us saying
to the world, this is what we believe, it

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:43.539
becomes very difficult for oppressed people
within the country to appeal to a conscience.

00:23:43.539 --> 00:23:47.599
To say, if you say you believe that, you must
act in that way.

00:23:47.599 --> 00:23:50.659
And it seems to me that that’s the importance
of the Declaration.

00:23:50.659 --> 00:23:54.470
Jefferson didn’t always do it, but the fact
that he put this declaration there makes it

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.239
possible for us to have a touchstone.

00:23:56.239 --> 00:23:59.639
Peter Onuf: I think there’s another dimension
of Jeffersonian thought having to do with

00:23:59.639 --> 00:24:05.889
equality that relates to this, and that is
the equality that Jefferson hoped to achieve,

00:24:05.889 --> 00:24:10.210
as I mentioned before, was the equality of
the American colonies.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:14.570
And that notion of equality applied to states
and to nations is I think probably the most

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:16.230
significant Jeffersonian legacy.

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:19.840
On the level of rhetoric, it’s human equality
that we refer to.

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:24.750
But it’s really national self-determination,
nation-making, the independence of states

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:30.889
that’s been, I think, the great legacy of
the American Revolution and of Jefferson’s

00:24:30.889 --> 00:24:31.889
Declaration.

00:24:31.889 --> 00:24:32.889
Ben Wattenberg: Okay.

00:24:32.889 --> 00:24:37.160
Thank you, Professor Peter Onuf, Professor
James Horton, Professor Jan Ellen Lewis, Judge

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:38.600
Robert Bork.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:39.919
And thank you.

00:24:39.919 --> 00:24:42.289
We have appreciated hearing from you very
much.

00:24:42.289 --> 00:24:47.100
Please send any comments or questions to the
address on the screen.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:00.900
For “Think Tank,” I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:32.800
